mcu_timer: skip cycles on which no timer can do anything

TIMER_Clock() advances timer.cycles one at a time, and at 12 MCU cycles per
emulated instruction that is six iterations per instruction. Almost all of
them do nothing: TIMER_ClockFrt() and TIMER_ClockTmr() both return
immediately unless timer.cycles sits on their divider edge. Callgrind put
TIMER_Clock at 29% of retired instructions on a 32-slot workload, ahead of
PCM_Update.

Every entry in both step tables is 2^k - 1, so every divider period is a
power of two and the smallest divides all the others. The set of cycles on
which any timer fires is therefore exactly the multiples of the smallest
period: no search is needed, just step to the next multiple. tcr is only
written by TIMER_Write(), which the MCU cannot reach while we are inside this
function, so the periods are computed once per call rather than per iteration.

Two things this patch is careful about, both learned the hard way:

  - A tmr step mask of 0 means "never step" and must not be counted as an
    edge. Mishandling exactly that is what commit 8d6f67a had to fix.

  - The loop must stop on the same cycle the original did, not on the next
    multiple of the period. timer.cycles persists across calls, and the
    skipped cycles are only known to be uneventful under the divider settings
    in force right now -- if the MCU programs a finer divider before the next
    call, cycles we ran past would have become live ones. An earlier draft
    overshot and was wrong in precisely that way.

Measured on x86-64 against a synthetic 32-slot PCM load: +11% end to end,
with TIMER_Clock's retired instruction count falling from 2295M to 2142M.
That second number is the one that matters for a Pi: an earlier draft that
computed the step per iteration was 10% faster in wall-clock terms on x86
while retiring *more* instructions than the original, which an in-order
Cortex-A53 would very likely have run slower.

patches/tests/timer_step_equivalence.cpp models the loop and checks all 1024
divider configurations over 9.3M cases, comparing both the set of cycles the
timers are clocked on and the final timer.cycles. No ROMs needed. It rejects
the overshooting draft, so it is known to be capable of failing.

Still worth confirming against real ROM audio before trusting it in
production; see patches/README.md.

--- a/src/backend/mcu_timer.cpp
+++ b/src/backend/mcu_timer.cpp
@@ -378,7 +378,44 @@
 
 void TIMER_Clock(mcu_timer_t& timer, uint64_t cycles)
 {
-    while (timer.cycles * 2 < cycles) // FIXME
+    // TIMER_ClockFrt() and TIMER_ClockTmr() both return immediately unless
+    // timer.cycles sits on their divider edge, so most of the iterations below
+    // do nothing at all. At 12 MCU cycles per instruction this loop runs six
+    // times per emulated instruction; skipping the empty ones is most of the
+    // work this function does.
+    //
+    // Every entry in both step tables is 2^k - 1, so every divider period is a
+    // power of two and the smallest one divides all the others. The set of
+    // cycles on which any timer fires is therefore exactly the multiples of the
+    // smallest period -- no per-iteration search needed, just step to the next
+    // multiple of it.
+    //
+    // A tmr step mask of 0 means "never step" and must not be mistaken for an
+    // edge; mishandling that is what commit 8d6f67a had to fix.
+    uint64_t period = ~(uint64_t)0;
+
+    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++)
+    {
+        const uint64_t mask =
+            timer.frt_step_table[timer.frt[i].tcr & (FRT_TCR_CKS0 | FRT_TCR_CKS1)];
+        if (mask + 1 < period)
+            period = mask + 1;
+    }
+
+    const uint64_t tmr_mask =
+        timer.tmr_step_table[timer.tmr.tcr & (TMR_TCR_CKS0 | TMR_TCR_CKS1 | TMR_TCR_CKS2)];
+    if (tmr_mask != 0 && tmr_mask + 1 < period)
+        period = tmr_mask + 1;
+
+    // First cycle count at which the original loop would have stopped. We must
+    // land on exactly this value and no further: the skipped cycles are only
+    // known to be uneventful under the divider settings in force right now, and
+    // if the MCU programs a finer divider before the next call, cycles we ran
+    // past would have become live ones. Overshooting here is a real, if narrow,
+    // behavioural change.
+    const uint64_t limit = (cycles + 1) / 2;
+
+    while (timer.cycles < limit)
     {
         for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++)
         {
@@ -387,7 +424,9 @@
 
         TIMER_ClockTmr(timer);
 
-        ++timer.cycles;
+        // Next multiple of period, which is period away when already aligned.
+        const uint64_t next = timer.cycles + period - (timer.cycles & (period - 1));
+        timer.cycles = next < limit ? next : limit;
     }
 }
 
